Z||000005||EdsB52||10-09-2001||03:09 AM||||I have a Garrett T-3 off an 84 Mustang 2.3 from a junkyard. I was going to go with a 3.4 turbo set-up, until I happened accross a 99 DeVille rollover for 3K, so I abandoned the turbo idea. It has an intake AR of .60 and an exhaust AR of .63. The turbine vanes have never contacted the sides and it is in overall great condition with no apparent cracks. I removed it off the motor, so I got the oil return line and evrything from the exhaust flange to the rubber hose at the intake. I have in it and I want $120 plus shipping.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-09-2001||09:06 PM||||turbo? Air cooled Motor? Only if it's profesionally done and even then only at low boost. And it will have to be intercooled precisely as well. One small mistake and the detonation will knock the motor apart.<P>Even in the water cooled VW/Porche motors this would be tricky to get right. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||10-09-2001||09:53 PM||||They built thousands of non intercooled flat 6 Turbo corvairs in the 60's. All have a basic turbo setup With the carb feeding into the turbo. Simple and effective<P>Are you turbo-ing a dual port motor? There used to be kits to Turbo a VW flat 4<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nashco||10-09-2001||09:59 PM||||I used to be HEAVY into VWs a from about four years ago to about a year ago.  There are some awesome kits for decent prices in the Hot VWs magazine, and I highly recommend going with a manufactured kit.  I seem to recall Turbo City (?) having a sweet injected turbo setup for almost a low enough price to not make it worth your while and risk to make your own setup.  Just as a sidenote, I wouldn't recommend going with a carbureted turbo setup, especially on the VWs...too many variables and not enough rigidity for the strong mounts needed, in my experience.  Not only do you have additional weight of a turbo hanging out somewhere, but you would then have a carb setup as well, making any robust design a major feat.  Also, if you plan on doing a turbo, make sure to do a thorough rebuild with quality parts.  There are tons of quality, affordable parts for the aircooled motors; it'll save ya a ton of money to do a good rebuild before installing a turbo, rather than after destroying one <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Good luck.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
